        <!-- Closing CTA -->
        <div class="mt-16 bg-gray-900 text-white rounded-2xl p-8">
          <h2 class="text-2xl font-black text-white mb-4">The Bottom Line</h2>
          <p class="text-gray-300 leading-relaxed mb-4">
            <strong class="text-white">For most home gyms, buy the REP AB-3000 2.0</strong>: every angle
            most people use, a 1,000 lb rating and a pad at the standard 17-inch height. If you mainly
            press flat with a barbell, the FB-5000 is steadier and cheaper; if the bench must disappear
            between sessions, the Bowflex 5.1S stands in a corner; and on a tight budget the FLYBIRD
            folds away and the Marcy covers dumbbell work for about $90.
          </p>
          <p class="text-gray-300 leading-relaxed mb-4">
            Whatever you buy, read the capacity on the maker's terms, check the pad height against the
            shortest person who will use it, and measure the rack before the bench arrives. Then put it
            on a firm floor and press inside the safeties —
            <a href="/best-squat-racks-for-home-gym/" class="text-[#5AA9FF] font-bold no-underline">our squat rack guide</a>
            covers both.
          </p>
